\details{
\subsection{How it works}{

The NbClust package implements many of the indices proposed in the
literature to determine the number of clusters. For each k from \code{k_min} to
\code{k_max}, the data is clustered and every index is computed. Each index then
proposes its best k according to its own rule (e.g. maximum, minimum or
largest change), and the proposals are counted. The default \code{index = "all"}
computes 26 indices: KL, CH, Hartigan, CCC, Scott, Marriot, TrCovW,
TraceW, Friedman, Rubin, Cindex, DB, Silhouette, Duda, PseudoT2, Beale,
Ratkowsky, Ball, PtBiserial, Frey, McClain, Dunn, Hubert, SDindex, Dindex
and SDbw. \code{"alllong"} adds Gap, Gamma, Gplus and Tau (much slower).

Most indices compare the dispersion \emph{within} clusters with the dispersion
\emph{between} clusters, in different ways. Examples:
\itemize{
\item \strong{CH} (Calinski-Harabasz, higher is better): ratio of the between- to
the within-cluster dispersion, corrected for the number of clusters.
\item \strong{DB} (Davies-Bouldin, lower is better): average similarity of each
cluster with its most similar other cluster.
\item \strong{Silhouette} (higher is better): see \code{\link[=seg_nbclust_silhouette]{seg_nbclust_silhouette()}}.
\item \strong{Duda}, \strong{PseudoT2}, \strong{Beale}: test whether a cluster should be
split into two; these can also suggest k = 1 (no clusters).
}

See Charrad et al. (2014) for the definitions of all indices.

The Hubert and D index are graphical methods without a direct proposal
(shown as k = 0 in the plot); some indices can fail for a given dataset.
Both are not counted, so the number of votes is often lower than 26.
Proposals outside \code{k_min} to \code{k_max} (such as k = 1) are not counted for
the optimal k either, but are shown in the plot. The console output and
the plots that NbClust produces itself are suppressed.

With a single \code{index} (e.g. \code{"ch"} or \code{"silhouette"}), there is no vote:
the values of the index per k are plotted instead, and the optimal k is the
best value of that index.
}

\subsection{Interpretation}{
\itemize{
\item A k supported by a clear majority of the indices is a strong candidate.
\item Votes spread over several values of k (common for survey data) indicate
that the data has no clear cluster structure; consider all values of k
with many votes as candidates.
\item Many indices are biased towards small numbers of clusters, so k = 2 often
receives many votes. Also look at the second most supported k.
}
}
